From 2739650ddd16e94d4ae69a3dbd476a292330cf66 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brian <brian@localhost.localdomain>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 08:32:32 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] gdn: guard three exponent overflows on inactive lanes and
 split-form halves

Found serving Qwen3.8-27B (TP2, gfx1201) on vllm-radiance 0.7.4. Each one turns
into 0 * INF = NaN, and the gated-delta-net output carries it into one whole
head_v-wide head of the layer, which destroys the model.

1. kkt_solve, padding rows. `gi` is forced to 0 for i >= rows while gb[j] keeps
   its real negative cumsum, so d = gi - gb[j] = -gb[j] is large POSITIVE and
   __expf overflows -- the opposite of the "never positive" invariant the code
   comments assert, which holds only for live rows. The NaNs land in padding
   rows of the 64x64 tile, and the blocked inverse merges the whole tile with
   WMMA, so they reach live rows. Adding `live &&` to the guard takes A from 16
   non-finite values to 0.

2. chunk_scan, split-form halves. e^{g_i-c}.e^{c-g_j} with cref at the chunk
   midpoint gives each half +/-(gate span)/2, so a span past ~176 sends one half
   to +INF while the other underflows to 0. kkt_solve's own header documents
   this hazard as its reason for using the direct form; chunk_scan kept the
   split form without a guard.

3. chunk_scan, V' staging (the dominant one, and only visible across chunks).
   The binding limit is not fp32: V' = V.gv[t] is staged in bf16, so gv must
   leave room for V under bf16's 3.4e38 ceiling. Clamping at e^88 is still too
   generous and NaNs; e^80 leaves ~3x margin.

The product is always safe -- rs[i].gv[j] = scale.e^{g_i-g_j} and the per-token
state weight gv[t].lam = e^{gl-g_t} are both <= 1 under the tril mask -- so
saturating the growing half keeps the shrinking one at 0 and the product
evaluates to 0, which is correct: e^{g_i-g_j} really is negligible whenever a
half leaves range. Ordinary chunks are untouched.

WikiText-2, 208,539 tokens, no other mitigation:

  stock                653586.39   top-1  0.00%
  fixed, clamp 70           8.3841       54.068%
  fixed, clamp 80           8.3706       54.119%
  fixed, clamp 83           8.3728       54.093%
  0.5.8 reference (no NaN)  8.3335       54.18%

The clamp bounds the damage rather than removing the cause. The clean fix is to
stop splitting a per-token weight that is provably <= 1 into a huge x tiny pair:
stage V' in fp32, or scale by e^{gl-g_t} directly on the state path.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 r4d_gdn_chunk_scan_k128_v128_c64_bf16.hip | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++--
 r4d_gdn_kkt_solve_k128_c64_bf16.hip       | 11 +++++++++--
 2 files changed, 29 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/r4d_gdn_chunk_scan_k128_v128_c64_bf16.hip b/r4d_gdn_chunk_scan_k128_v128_c64_bf16.hip
index 1835fba..e18d5d8 100644
--- a/r4d_gdn_chunk_scan_k128_v128_c64_bf16.hip
+++ b/r4d_gdn_chunk_scan_k128_v128_c64_bf16.hip
@@ -467,8 +467,26 @@ __global__ __launch_bounds__(NTHR) void r4d_gdn_chunk_scan_kernel(
       bool ok = tid < nval;
       s.bt[tid] = btr;
       s.ge[tid] = __expf(ggr);
-      s.gv[tid] = ok ? __expf(cref - ggr) : 0.f;
-      s.rs[tid] = ok ? scale * __expf(ggr - cref) : 0.f;
+      // The split form e^{g_i-c}.e^{c-g_j} is one exp per token instead of eight per tile, but
+      // each half can overflow on its own even where the PRODUCT cannot. The binding limit is not
+      // fp32: it is that V' = V.gv[t] is staged in bf16 (s.D), so gv must leave room for V and for
+      // the 128-term accumulation of V'^T @ K under bf16's 3.4e38 ceiling. e^70 = 2.5e30 leaves
+      // headroom against |V| and bf16's 3.4e38 ceiling; e^88 does not, which is why clamping
+      // there still produced NaN. 80 is measured, not assumed -- WikiText-2 PPL over 208,539
+      // tokens with no other mitigation: 70 -> 8.3841, 80 -> 8.3706, 83 -> 8.3728, stock -> 653586.
+      // Past 80 the marginal overflow costs more than the extra range recovers.
+      // Specifically: with cref at the chunk
+      // midpoint each half spans +/-(gate span)/2, so a span past ~176 sends one half to +INF
+      // while the other underflows to 0, and 0 * INF = NaN.
+      //
+      // The product is always safe -- rs[i].gv[j] = scale.e^{g_i-g_j}, and g decreases while the
+      // tril mask keeps only j <= i, so the exponent is never positive. Saturating the growing
+      // half just below fp32's overflow point keeps it finite, and the shrinking half still
+      // underflows to 0, so the product evaluates to 0 -- which is the correct answer, because
+      // e^{g_i-g_j} really is negligible whenever a half runs out of range. Where both halves fit
+      // (every ordinary chunk) nothing changes.
+      s.gv[tid] = ok ? __expf(fminf(cref - ggr, 80.0f)) : 0.f;
+      s.rs[tid] = ok ? scale * __expf(fminf(ggr - cref, 80.0f)) : 0.f;
     }
     BAR();                                                   // (1)
 #if PFIN && PFV == 4
diff --git a/r4d_gdn_kkt_solve_k128_c64_bf16.hip b/r4d_gdn_kkt_solve_k128_c64_bf16.hip
index 4538f61..d259fbc 100644
--- a/r4d_gdn_kkt_solve_k128_c64_bf16.hip
+++ b/r4d_gdn_kkt_solve_k128_c64_bf16.hip
@@ -207,8 +207,15 @@ void r4d_gdn_kkt_solve_kernel(const unsigned short* __restrict__ k,
 #pragma unroll
         for (int e = 0; e < 8; ++e) {
           const int j = j0 + e;
-          // i > j means g_i <= g_j, so this exponent is never positive whatever the span is
-          const float d = (i > j && j < rows) ? (gi - s.gb[j]) : -INFINITY;
+          // i > j means g_i <= g_j, so this exponent is never positive whatever the span is --
+          // but ONLY for a live row. On a padding row (i >= rows) gi is forced to 0 above while
+          // gb[j] keeps its real, negative cumsum, so gi - gb[j] = -gb[j] is large POSITIVE,
+          // __expf overflows to +INF, and bi * INF = 0 * INF = NaN. Those NaNs sit in padding
+          // rows of the 64x64 tile, and the blocked inverse below merges the whole tile with
+          // WMMA, so they reach live rows and poison the chunk's output for the head.
+          // Measured on Qwen3.8-27B at TP2: 16 non-finite values in A, which chunk_scan then
+          // spreads to one entire head_v-wide head of the layer output.
+          const float d = (live && i > j && j < rows) ? (gi - s.gb[j]) : -INFINITY;
           vv[e] = bi * __expf(d) * P[t][e];
         }
         *(uint2*)(&s.A[i][j0])     = f2bf4(vv[0], vv[1], vv[2], vv[3]);
-- 
2.54.0

